#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# random_choice

#% text
# `random_choice(xs)` gives a uniformly chosen element of the list `xs`; `random_choice(xs, n)` gives `n` of them, with replacement.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
seed(3); random_choice([a, b, c])
#% output
#= c

#% cell
seed(3); random_choice(["H", "T"], 10)
#% output
#= ["T", "T", "T", "T", "H", "T", "H", "H", "H", "H"]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Each element comes up about equally often:

#% cell
seed(4); counts(random_choice([1, 2, 3], 30000))
#% output
#= assoc(3 -> 9979, 2 -> 10091, 1 -> 9930)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `random_sample`, `random_integer`, `seed`
